# Recipe Box API test requests, Week 11
#
# VS Code: install the "REST Client" extension (by Huachao Mao),
# then click "Send Request" above any block below.
# Thunder Client works too: copy the method, URL, headers and body.
#
# Tuesday: start the server first (npm run dev or npm run cp14).
# Thursday: change @base to your Vercel production URL and run them all again.

@base = http://localhost:3000
# @base = https://your-project.vercel.app

### Health check (Thursday, checkpoint 15 and later): expect 200 and runningOn
GET {{base}}/api/health

### API info
GET {{base}}/

### Read: every recipe
GET {{base}}/api/recipes

### Read: one recipe by id
GET {{base}}/api/recipes/3

### Read: an id that does not exist (expect 404)
GET {{base}}/api/recipes/99

### Read: an id that is not a number (expect 404 from checkId, NOT a 500)
GET {{base}}/api/recipes/two

### Create: a good recipe (expect 201 with a new id)
POST {{base}}/api/recipes
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Ramen",
  "cuisine": "Japanese",
  "minutes": 40,
  "tags": ["noodles"]
}

### Create: a bad recipe (expect 400 with a list of problems)
POST {{base}}/api/recipes
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "",
  "minutes": "forty"
}

### Create: broken JSON (expect 400 "Request body is not valid JSON")
POST {{base}}/api/recipes
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "oops

### Update: change one field (expect 200)
PATCH {{base}}/api/recipes/2
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "minutes": 45
}

### Delete (expect 204, then run it again for a 404)
DELETE {{base}}/api/recipes/4

### Filters: vegetarian and 30 minutes or less
GET {{base}}/api/recipes?tag=vegetarian&maxMinutes=30

### A route that does not exist (expect JSON 404)
GET {{base}}/api/desserts

######## CORS EXPERIMENTS (Tuesday) ########
# These requests add an Origin header by hand, the way a browser would.
# Thunder Client and REST Client never BLOCK anything: they just show you
# the headers. Look for Access-Control-Allow-Origin in each response.

### Pretend to be the page on 5173 (in CORS_ORIGINS): the header comes back
GET {{base}}/api/recipes
Origin: http://localhost:5173

### Pretend to be a page on 5174 (NOT in the list): still a 200 and still the data,
### but no Access-Control-Allow-Origin header. A browser would hide this response.
GET {{base}}/api/recipes
Origin: http://localhost:5174

### The preflight a browser sends before a JSON POST (expect 204 and Allow headers)
OPTIONS {{base}}/api/recipes
Origin: http://localhost:5173
Access-Control-Request-Method: POST
Access-Control-Request-Headers: content-type
